Bursitis (Trochanteric/Olecranon/Prepatellar)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Focal fluctuant swelling, Tenderness to direct pressure, Preserved joint range of motion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-010
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, rapid swelling of the anterior knee causing extreme pain on direct pressure and inability to walk or kneel.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage determined the patient developed a rapid, localized, highly painful, and warm fluctuant mass over the knee within 24 hours. Urgent physical assessment was critical to differentiate between benign inflammatory prepatellar bursitis and joint-destroying septic arthritis or cellulitis, and to initiate appropriate localized therapy prior to morning clinical services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My kneecap is swollen like a water balloon; it's burning hot and I can't even let my trousers touch it."
History of Present Illness: Patient (a carpet layer) presents with acute onset of severe localized swelling over the anterior aspect of the right knee. Timeline: Noticed mild tenderness yesterday; rapidly progressed over the last 12 hours into a large, focal, well-demarcated fluctuant mass directly over the prepatellar bursa. Pain is triggered and significantly worsened by any direct pressure, rendering the patient completely unable to kneel or wear tight clothing. Reports exquisite point tenderness upon accidental palpation. Patient exhibits an antalgic gait to avoid any skin tension over the knee. Denies systemic fevers or rigors.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a severe limp, unable to perform occupational duties or basic bending.
Social/Safety Concerns: Occupational impact; patient unable to work.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/80 | HR: 86 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing systemically, dressed in shorts to avoid fabric contact with the knee.
Physical Exam: Right knee demonstrates a focal, well-demarcated, fluctuant, fluid-filled swelling located strictly anterior to the patella. Exquisite point tenderness upon direct, light palpation of the swollen bursa. Mild overlying erythema and localized warmth. Crucially, active and passive range of motion of the actual knee joint (flexion/extension) is intact and painless throughout the primary arc, with pain only occurring at extreme flexion when the skin over the bursa is maximally stretched. No joint line tenderness. Ligaments stable. No spreading demarcated erythema beyond the bursa.
Environmental Observations: Tools and work equipment visible; reinforcing the repetitive mechanical trauma mechanism.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Prepatellar Bursitis (Non-septic) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out clinically by the preserved, fundamentally painless arc of motion of the knee joint itself, lack of deep joint effusion, and absence of systemic fever.

Cellulitis - Ruled out due to swelling and erythema being strictly confined to the anatomical boundaries of the prepatellar bursa without spreading, irregular borders.

Patellar Fracture - Ruled out by normal, painless active straight leg raise (intact extensor mechanism) and mechanism being repetitive microtrauma rather than a discrete high-impact blow.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, rapid-onset swelling and extreme tenderness localized strictly to the prepatellar bursa following occupational mechanical overload. The key clinical distinction was between a superficial bursal inflammation and a deep, joint-destroying septic arthritis. Postponing evaluation until the next in-hours period risked exposing the patient to the devastating consequences of delayed treatment for a septic joint, or severe, unmanaged pain. Urgent after-hours assessment firmly established the superficial, non-articular nature of the pathology—evidenced by a painless, intact internal joint range of motion—bypassing the need for emergency joint aspiration and safely initiating potent conservative anti-inflammatory management in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food for 7 days. Applied a compressive elastic bandage to the knee.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Use thick, specialized gel knee pads whenever resuming any floor-based activities. Consequence: Elimination of direct friction and compression on the bursa, preventing the painful fluid sack from refilling (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply cold packs over the compression bandage for 15 minutes, three times a day. Consequence: Noticeable reduction in the local "burning" sensation and visible shrinkage of the swelling (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days. If the area becomes significantly redder, hotter, or fevers develop, diagnostic aspiration may be required to definitively exclude infection.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops systemic fevers, chills, if the redness begins spreading rapidly down the leg, or if the internal knee joint itself becomes too painful to bend.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; while currently presenting as mechanical inflammation, superficial bursitis can become secondarily infected. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The fluid will slowly reabsorb over 1-3 weeks with strict avoidance of kneeling and continuous compression. Re-injury is highly likely if biomechanics are not altered.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency care if you develop a fever or the shivers, if the redness spreads down your shin, or if the deep joint of the knee becomes agonizing to move.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The fluid sac over your kneecap has become severely inflamed from repetitive kneeling. The compression bandage physically squashes the sac to prevent more fluid from entering, while the ibuprofen reduces the chemical burning sensation.
Actionable Steps: Do not kneel on this leg under any circumstances until the swelling is completely gone. Keep the compression bandage firm during the day to physically squeeze the fluid out of the space.

